If you're working on a system where the default timeout for running tests is set too high, you may need to adjust the timeout setting for your specific testing framework or environment.

For example, in Pytest, you can set a global timeout for all tests in your entire project:

```python
import pytest

@pytest.mark.timeout(1)  # Set the timeout to 1 second
def test_fast_sum():
    assert fast_sum([4, 91, 17, 3, 56]) == 171
    assert fast_sum([820, 13, 447, 9, 601, 72]) == 1962
    assert fast_sum([5, 5, 5, 5]) == 20
```

In JUnit, you can set a timeout for individual tests:

```java
public class MyTest {
    @Test(timeout = 1000)  // Set the timeout to 1 second
    public void testFastSum() {
        assertEquals(171, fastSum(new int[]{4, 91, 17, 3, 56}));
        assertEquals(1962, fastSum(new int[]{820, 13, 447, 9, 601, 72}));
        assertEquals(20, fastSum(new int[]{5, 5, 5, 5}));
    }
}
```

Or in Node.js, you can use `async/await` and `setTimeout` to set a timeout for the main function:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

function slowFunction() {
    return new Promise((resolve) => {
        setTimeout(() => resolve("Hello, World!"), 2000);  // Simulate a slow operation
    });
}

async function main() {
    try {
        console.log(await slowFunction());  // Wait for 2 seconds
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
    }
}

main();
